    "type": "mc",
    "prompt": "A walrus and a polar bear had a swimming competition around an iceberg. They jumped into the water at the same time. One full lap around the iceberg took the bear half an hour, and it took the walrus 6 minutes less. The competition ended when one animal was one lap ahead of the other. How many minutes did the competition take?",
    "image": null,
    "choices": [
      {
        "key": "A",
        "text": "48"
      },
      {
        "key": "B",
        "text": "96"
      },
      {
        "key": "C",
        "text": "120"
      },
      {
        "key": "D",
        "text": "150"
      },
      {
        "key": "E",
        "text": "180"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "C",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Laps per hour",
        "body": "The bear needs 30 minutes per lap and the walrus 24 minutes. In 120 minutes (the least common multiple of 24 and 30) the walrus swims 5 laps and the bear 4 laps, so the walrus is exactly one lap ahead. Before that the gap is less than one lap, since it grows steadily.\n\n**Answer: (C) 120**"
      },
      {
        "title": "Equation",
        "body": "After $t$ minutes the walrus has swum $\\frac{t}{24}$ laps and the bear $\\frac{t}{30}$ laps. We need $\\frac{t}{24} - \\frac{t}{30} = 1$, i.e. $\\frac{t}{120} = 1$, so $t = 120$.\n\n**Answer: (C) 120**"
      }
    ],
    "topics": [
      "rates"
    ],
    "difficulty": 2,
