    ],
    "answer": "D",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Three hexagons",
        "body": "The shape consists of three hexagons, each made of 6 small triangles, and every pair of hexagons shares one edge; the three shared edges meet at the centre point. Every tile covers two neighbouring triangles.\n\n**Case 1: no tile crosses a shared edge.** Then each hexagon is tiled on its own. The 6 triangles of a hexagon form a ring, and a ring of 6 can be split into neighbouring pairs in exactly 2 ways. That gives $2 \\times 2 \\times 2 = 8$ tilings.\n\n**Case 2: some tile crosses a shared edge.** Each hexagon has 6 triangles; a tile crossing out of it uses one of them, so an even number of crossing tiles must touch each hexagon (otherwise an odd number of triangles is left inside it). Each hexagon touches only two shared edges, so either both or none of its shared edges are crossed. If one shared edge is crossed, both its hexagons need their other shared edge crossed as well, so all three shared edges are crossed. Then each hexagon loses its two triangles at the centre, leaving a strip of 4 triangles, which can be tiled in exactly 1 way. That gives 1 tiling.\n\nTotal: $8 + 1 = 9$.\n\n**Answer: (D) 9**"
      }
    ],
