      }
    ],
    "answer": "D",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Gain per removed cube",
        "body": "The big cube has surface $6 \\cdot 16 = 96$. We need $1.5 \\cdot 96 = 144$, i.e. a gain of 48.\n\nWhen we take out a small cube that has $k$ faces on the outside, those $k$ faces disappear and its other $6-k$ faces become new surface: the change is $6 - 2k$. A cube we can take out has at least one outside face, so each removal gains at most $6 - 2 = 4$. Hence at least $48 \\div 4 = 12$ cubes are needed.\n\nTwelve is enough: take out 2 of the 4 centre cubes on each of the 6 faces, choosing two that do not touch (diagonally opposite). Each such cube has exactly 1 outside face and all its other neighbours stay in place, so each gains 4: total $12 \\cdot 4 = 48$.\n\n**Answer: (D) 12**"
      }
    ],
